{"term": "Drecksau", "lang": "de", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "filth_insult", "concept": "scumbag", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 80, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Filthy pig or dirty swine; Dreck (dirt/filth) + Sau (sow); a strong insult for a disgusting, morally filthy, or contemptible person.", "english_term": "filthy pig", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "No direct sexual denotation; the bodily-filth register and sow (Sau) reference give it an animalistic disgust valence; used for both genders; stronger than Saukerl because Dreck (filth) intensifies Sau."}
{"term": "Pisser", "lang": "de", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "urine_insult", "concept": "bastard_insult", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 60, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Pisser or weakling; from pissen (to urinate); insult implying a person is a crybaby, coward, or contemptible weakling who would wet themselves under pressure.", "english_term": "pisser", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "In German usage the insult foregrounds cowardice and weakness rather than general contempt; milder than Arschloch or Wichser; urination as a marker of loss of control under fear is the operative image."}
