{"term": "Schweinhund", "lang": "de", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "filth_insult", "concept": "bastard_insult", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "dated_slang", "crude": 60, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 95, "recognition_pct_peak": 95, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Pig-dog; Schwein (pig) + Hund (dog); classic compound insult for a despicable or villainous person; has acquired a theatrical or ironic quality in 2026.", "english_term": "scoundrel", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Internationally recognizable due to WWII-era fiction and films using it as a stereotypical German villain expletive; contemporary usage is often ironic or humorous; still lands as a genuine insult when deployed without irony."}
{"term": "Pissnelke", "lang": "de", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "urine_insult", "concept": "bitch_insult", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 68, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Piss carnation; Piss + Nelke (carnation flower); creative compound insult primarily directed at women, implying petty contemptibility; the dainty flower ironically amplifies the vulgarity.", "english_term": "pissy cow", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Less standardized than core insults; the flower suffix creates a mocking, diminishing quality—as if calling someone decoratively worthless; recognition lower among younger speakers who prefer newer insult compounds."}
